\section{Hamiltonian Mechanics and Autopoietic Extensions}

\subsection{Classical Hamiltonian Systems}
A classical Hamiltonian system evolves according to:
\begin{equation}
\dot{q}_i = \pdv{H}{p_i}, \qquad
\dot{p}_i = -\pdv{H}{q_i},
\end{equation}
where $H(q,p)$ is the Hamiltonian function defined on a symplectic manifold $(\mathcal{M},\omega)$.

The symplectic form $\omega$ satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\omega = \sum_i dq_i \wedge dp_i,
\end{equation}
ensuring conservation of phase-space volume (Liouville's theorem).

\subsection{Autopoietic Hamiltonian Perturbations}
Autopoietic systems introduce non-conservative terms:
\begin{equation}
\dot{q}_i = \pdv{H}{p_i} + \Phi_i(q,p,t),
\qquad
\dot{p}_i = -\pdv{H}{q_i} + \Psi_i(q,p,t),
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_i$ and $\Psi_i$ encode adaptive, self-modifying feedback.

These terms violate strict symplectic conservation:
\begin{equation}
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{p} \neq 0,
\end{equation}
enabling ontological restructuring and metamorphic evolution.

